import RequestProject.Wikidata.Rdf

/-!
# Blank nodes and simple entailment

`Wikidata/Rdf.lean` models the RDF layers of a knowledge base with *ground*
triples only: every position of every triple is a name.  Real RDF has a second
kind of term, the **blank node**, and it is the only genuinely logical device in
the data model — a blank node is an existential variable, so a graph is read as
the existential closure of its triples.

This module adds that layer and proves the classical facts about it.

* **Syntax and substitution.**  `BTerm`, `BTriple`, `BGraph`, the instance
  mappings `Subst` that send blank nodes to terms, and the two composition laws
  (`substGraph_comp`, `substGraph_id`) that make instantiation functorial.
* **Semantics.**  A *model* is a graph of the interpretation's own terms, and
  `Models I g` says the existential closure of `g` holds in `I`.  `Entails g e`
  is the model-theoretic relation: every model of `g` is a model of `e`.
* **The interpolation lemma** (`entails_iff_instance_subset`).  Model-theoretic
  simple entailment coincides with a purely syntactic condition — `g` entails `e`
  exactly when some instance of `e` is a subgraph of `g`.  The proof is the usual
  one: the canonical model of `g` is `g` itself.
* **The consequences**: reflexivity, transitivity, monotonicity in the data
  (`entails_mono`), and the fact that on ground graphs entailment collapses to
  containment (`ground_entails_iff_subset`).
* **What blank nodes do and do not buy.**  A blank node may be introduced for any
  term of a recorded triple (`entails_blank_generalisation`), but it is not a
  witness: the existential does not entail any of its instances
  (`blank_is_not_a_witness`).  Consequently entailment does *not* distribute over
  the union of two graphs that share a blank node
  (`entails_union_needs_standardising_apart`) — this is exactly why the RDF merge
  operation standardises blank nodes apart — and two equivalent graphs can have
  different sizes (`redundant_blank_is_removable`).
* **Leanness** (`IsLean`).  A graph is lean when no proper subgraph entails it.
  Ground graphs are lean (`ground_isLean`) and the two-blank graph above is not
  (`twoBlanks_not_isLean`).
* **The merge** (`merge`, `entails_merge_iff`).  Standardising the blank nodes
  apart repairs the failure just described: a graph entails the merge of two
  graphs exactly when it entails each of them, so the merge — and not the
  concatenation — is the conjunction of two graphs in the entailment preorder.
* **A decision procedure** (`entailsCheck`, `entailsCheck_iff`, and the resulting
  `Decidable` instance): search the finitely many maps from the blank nodes of
  `e` into the terms of `g`.  Entailment of one recorded fragment by another is
  then closed by `decide`.
* **The bridge to the Wikidata layer**: the direct triples of a knowledge base
  embed into blank-node graphs (`entails_embed_directTriples`), and blanking the
  object of a truthy statement gives a consequence of the base
  (`kb_entails_blanked_object`) which does not itself pin the object down.

Unlike the SPARQL operators of `Wikidata/Sparql*.lean`, simple entailment is
monotone: new data never withdraws a consequence.
-/

